Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:05.990 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  hello everyone  and welcome to the first lecture on the  epistemology of fake news  okay so let's begin with the first  section  understanding the threat i can imagine  that  just like me many of you have been  following the news unusually closely  lately  and you'll no doubt have heard that  there's a lot of fake news about the  coronavirus  circulating online for example you may  read that bill gates is spreading the  coronavirus  in order to implant microchips in our  brains  you may read that the virus was  engineered as a bio weapon  and that it contains genetic sequences  inserted from hiv  or you may read that 5g mobile networks  are in some way implicated in the  pandemic  either by directly emitting the virus  through some kind of miasma  or by weakening the human immune system  through some kind of high frequency  electromagnetic radiation  if you come down with a cough a fever  and difficulty breathing  and turn to the internet in search of a  cure you face a bewildering array of  options  you may be encouraged to eat large  amounts of garlic ginger or hot peppers  to rub goose fat on your chest to expose  yourself to high temperatures or maybe  to expose yourself to low temperatures  to drink cow's urine or bathing cows  dung  if you had tuned in to alex jones of  infowars a little while ago you would  have been encouraged to gargle with  super blue  silver immune gargle which contains  colloidal silver  and of course as we heard only a few  days ago donald  trump has been taking hydroxychloroquine  a drug developed for the treatment of  malaria  but which still has not been fully  tested for its safety and efficacy in  treating covid19  the list of things i just mentioned is  varied ranging from wild conspiracy  theories to theories that are just  false misleading or lack sufficient  information  supporting evidence  but despite the variation i'm calling  reports of all of these things i just  mentioned fake news  that is to say i'm operating with a  particularly broad definition of the  term  and one that's probably at odds with the  way in which it's used by others  for example trump uses the term fake  news to express antipathy  towards any content that conflicts with  his own beliefs or interests  some might say that trump has tainted  the term by using it this way  but i've decided to stick to it because  this variability in its meaning  actually reflects some of the themes  that i want to discuss  so to be clear what i mean by fake news  is  publicly available content that presents  itself as reliable  factual information but is in fact false  misleading  or at least in some cases produced with  the intention to deceive  so satirical news media such as the  onion or the borowitz report which make  it clear that they're satirical  don't count as fake news by my  definition because they don't present  their content as reliable factual  information  uh public posts websites blogs and the  like all count as fake news if they do  present their content as factual even in  part  and if that content is false or  misleading of course they're going to be  borderline cases but this rough  definition will do  for our purposes what's important is  that fake news is presented as reliable  factual information  when it's really false or misleading  of course fake news has been around for  a long time but as we've all become  acutely aware  fake news travels farther and faster  online than it ever has been able to do  before  so you might ask what does epistemology  have to do with fake news  well epistemology is the philosophical  study of knowledge  and knowledge involves both true belief  and rationality  that is if you know a statement p then  it follows that you have a true belief  that p  and you have reached that belief in the  right kind of way that is in a way  that's rational or reliable  or is based appropriately on reasons or  evidence  if you believe something true but you  don't have any reasons or evidence for  your belief then your belief is just a  lucky guess  and doesn't amount to knowledge and what  i want to talk about today  are some of the threats fake news poses  towards knowledge  of course there is the obvious threat  that fake news poses towards knowledge  since you know something only if you  have a true belief  if you're misled by fake news into  believing something false  then obviously you lack knowledge but  there are  further less obvious ways in which fake  news poses a threat to knowledge  and i'm going to discuss three of these  less obvious threats to knowledge  which i've listed on your handout they  are the threat from irrationality  the threat from rational polarization  and the threat from  relativism so first let me say something  a little bit about the threat from  irrationality  a striking feature of our current  situation is that fake news seems to go  hand in hand with political polarization  when groups on either side of a  political spectrum move further and  further apart from one another  this kind of political polarization of  factual belief is hardly new to the u.s  where conservatives and liberals are  polarized over all sorts of empirical  matters  such as whether humans are a significant  cause of global warming  or whether concealed carry laws increase  gun crime  and similar patterns of polarization can  be found around the world  the thing is that one prominent  explanation for why we are so  susceptible to fake news  why we polarize in this way appeals to  human irrationality  but of course irrationality poses a  threat to knowledge  if we manage to form true beliefs but do  so in an irrational way  our true beliefs are only as good as  lucky guesses and luck is the enemy of  knowledge  this is the first threat i'm going to  discuss the threat posed by  irrationality however irrationality is  not the whole story because it turns out  that we can be misled by fake news  and we can be led to polarize even  though we're irrational  sorry even though we're rational this  might seem like good news from the point  of view of knowledge  but unfortunately it's not because if  rational people systematically polarize  over factual beliefs  which are either true or false this  implies that the  rules of rationality are not a  particularly reliable way of getting it  the truth  so the second threat to knowledge i will  discuss is the threat posed by  rational polarization  the final threat that fake news poses to  knowledge that i'd like to discuss  is the threat from relativism this  threat stems not so much from the fact  that there's a lot of fake news about  but from a prevalent philosophical  approach that people seem to have  towards fake news and towards truth and  knowledge more broadly  to use a cliche the attitude is  sometimes called post-truth  but it's really a crude version of  relativism and this connects with issues  that i've been grappling with since i  went to graduate school  an example of this kind of crude  relativism is expressed in the alt-right  expressed by the alt-right internet  troll mike cernovich  in an interview i happened to read in  the new yorker in 2016.  so cernovich had been spewing out  massive quantities of  misinformation in the run-up to the 2016  presidential election in the u.s  much of which was targeted at hillary  clinton  and in the interview cernovich basically  acknowledges that not all of which he  posts is true  or is has been carefully vetted and he  clearly doesn't think that the truth or  evidence matters  what matters he seems to think is just  getting the message  out there persuading people to take up  his point of view  completely regardless of whether what he  says is true or supported by evidence  and he justifies this by saying quote  look i read postmodernist theory in  college if everything is narrative then  we need  alternatives to the dominant narrative  so according to this crude relativism  there's nothing  there's no such thing as objective truth  reason or knowledge  no such thing as fake news objectively  speaking  rather what's fake news and what's real  news all depends on your perspective  since there is no objective truth all  you can do is fight your corner  get your own narrative out there and  persuade as many as you can to join your  team  success is measured in numbers of tweets  and retweets  what's surprising about this new  development is that relativism has for a  long time been the darling of the left  particularly prevalent among left-wing  intellectuals in the humanities  reading that new yorker article about  the alt-right in fact took me right back  to grad school  where relativism seemed to be part of  the furniture  i did my phd at the department of  history and philosophy of science in  cambridge  in the midst of the relativistic turn in  the study of science  and relativism i think still strikes  many on the left  as a very attractive view so in the  rest of this lecture and in the next  lecture i'm going to discuss  each of these threats to knowledge in  turn  but i will spend most of my time on the  third i think that there are many people  on the left who react to the crude  relativism of the alt-right  and who want to defend truth reason and  democracy from this scourge  but given the power of relativism over  the popular imagination  it may be hard to find arguments against  it so  if you're one of those people who finds  relativism attractive  i hope to convince you that it's not and  if you're one of those people who finds  relativism unattractive  hopefully what i can do today is help  you out with some arguments against it  to continue watching this video click  the link in the top left or in the  description below  or visit iai.tv for more debates and  talks from the world's leading thinkers  on today's biggest ideas

